Business Recommendations

Based on the customer churn analysis, Maven Communications should focus its retention strategy on customers who show the highest risk of churn and address the main reasons customers report for leaving.

1. Target new month-to-month customers with early retention offers

Customers with month-to-month contracts and 0–6 months of tenure had a churn rate of 57.06%, making this one of the highest-risk customer segments identified. Among these churned customers, the most common reasons were that competitors made better offers (18.72%) and had better devices (18.08%).

Recommendation: Maven Communications should introduce an early-retention program for new month-to-month customers. This could include competitive introductory offers, device promotions, and proactive check-ins during the first six months.

2. Review Offer E and improve its value proposition

Offer E had the highest overall churn rate at 52.92%. This suggests that customers receiving this offer are particularly vulnerable to cancellation.

Recommendation: Maven should investigate why Offer E customers are leaving and compare the offer's pricing, services, and benefits with competing offers. The company should consider redesigning Offer E or providing customers with a more attractive alternative before they churn.

3. Address competitive threats involving devices, pricing, and data

The most common churn reasons were "Competitor had better devices" (313 customers) and "Competitor made better offer" (311 customers). Other important competitive reasons included competitors offering more data (117 customers) and higher download speeds (100 customers).

Recommendation: Maven should regularly benchmark its device selection, pricing, data allowances, and download speeds against competitors. Improving these areas could make Maven's offerings more competitive and reduce customers' incentives to switch providers.

4. Strengthen customer support and technical assistance

A significant number of customers reported "Attitude of support person" (220 customers) and "Attitude of service provider" (94 customers) as reasons for leaving. In addition, customers without Online Security, Premium Tech Support, Online Backup, and Device Protection Plan had relatively high churn rates.

Recommendation: Maven should invest in customer-support training and evaluate the quality of its technical-support experience. The company could also proactively offer relevant support services to customers in high-risk segments.

5. Develop a churn-risk monitoring system

The analysis identified several high-risk characteristics, including month-to-month contracts, short tenure, Offer E, lack of Online Security, lack of Premium Tech Support, and Fiber Optic customers.
